<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!--
  SCOPE LIMIT — READ BEFORE TRUSTING THE <cloud-backup> SECTION BELOW.
  This app declares android:backupAgent=".util.AppBackupAgent" and does not set
  android:fullBackupOnly="true". AppBackupAgent extends BaseBackupAgent, which overrides
  onBackup()/onRestore() (library/src/main/java/com/vayunmathur/library/util/BackupAgent.kt:21,48),
  so cloud backup for this app runs through the KEY/VALUE transport and the <cloud-backup>
  section here is NOT consulted. What actually leaves the device is whatever the agent puts
  in its stream: today that is dbConfigs = listOf("passwords-db" to passphrase), which
  BackupHelper exports DECRYPTED via SqlCipherDbCodec. That is a plaintext vault going to
  Google, and no XML in this file can stop it. Fixing it requires a code change to the
  agent, or android:fullBackupOnly="true" to route cloud through these rules instead.

  The <device-transfer> section below IS effective: BaseBackupAgent does not override
  onFullBackup(), so the default BackupAgent.onFullBackup() runs for D2D and honours these
  rules. The <cloud-backup> section is kept deliberately, as correct-by-default policy for
  the day the agent is removed or fullBackupOnly is set.

  Deny-all. Overrides the permissive include-everything default in the shared :library module.

  Nothing this app persists is safe to copy off the device, and nothing it persists is
  restorable even if it were copied:

    databases/passwords-db (+ -wal/-shm/-journal/_temp)
        The vault. SQLCipher-encrypted; the passphrase is AES-GCM wrapped by the
        non-exportable AndroidKeyStore key "db_no_auth_key", which never leaves the
        device on either backup path. -wal/-shm are listed because WAL contents would
        otherwise leak plaintext pages independently of the main file. _temp is the
        plaintext staging file used during the plaintext->SQLCipher migration.
    files/kdbx-sync-backup.kdbx
        Pre-overwrite copy of the user's KDBX vault, protected only by the master
        password. Offline-crackable, so this must never reach Google's servers.
    shared_prefs/secure_prefs.xml, shared_prefs/kdbx_sync_prefs.xml
        Keystore-wrapped passphrase + IV, and the wrapped KDBX master password.
        Restoring these is actively harmful: DatabaseHelper.isKeyGenerated() only checks
        that the IV is present, so a restored prefs file with no matching Keystore key
        puts the app on the decrypt path with a key that does not exist.
    files/datastore_default.preferences_pb
        kdbx sync settings, including kdbx_sync_document_uri. The persisted SAF URI
        permission grant does not survive backup, so restoring the URI only produces a
        sync that fails against an unreadable document.
    databases/androidx.work.workdb (+ sidecars)
        Regenerable WorkManager scheduling state.

  Device transfer is denied too, not just cloud backup. D2D does not touch Google's
  servers, but it also does not migrate AndroidKeyStore material, so carrying the vault
  across yields an unopenable database and the corrupt-state trap described above. The
  supported migration path is the app's own KDBX sync to a user-chosen SAF document.
-->
<data-extraction-rules>
    <cloud-backup>
        <exclude domain="root" path="." />
        <exclude domain="file" path="." />
        <exclude domain="database" path="." />
        <exclude domain="sharedpref" path="." />
        <exclude domain="external" path="." />
    </cloud-backup>
    <device-transfer>
        <exclude domain="root" path="." />
        <exclude domain="file" path="." />
        <exclude domain="database" path="." />
        <exclude domain="sharedpref" path="." />
        <exclude domain="external" path="." />
    </device-transfer>
</data-extraction-rules>
